{"kind":"request","text":"A report filed through the MCP `state` tool can read as a review to every human and be no review at all to the fold, and nothing says so. Measured today, on a real approval.\n\nThe fold makes a report a review only when `body.verdict` is set: internal/workroom/fold.go, `state.Kind == KindReport \u0026\u0026 state.Body[\"verdict\"] != \"\" \u0026\u0026 record.decision.Verdict == Effective`. `gs merge` then checks `approval.Body[\"verdict\"] != \"approved\"`. The `gs review` command cannot get this wrong — cmd/gs/main.go builds the body as `{\"verdict\", \"head\", \"artifact\"}` and refuses any verdict that is not approved or changes-requested.\n\nThe MCP `state` tool has no such guard. I filed three reviews today with `body.status` instead of `body.verdict`, having previously filed forty-seven correctly. All three were admitted, all three were ruled effective, all three read as reviews in their text, and none of them projected as a review. The approval among them would have been refused at merge; the two changes-requested ones simply do not appear in the reviews projection at all, so the record under-reports the review work done on that candidate.\n\nWhat makes this the bad kind of failure is that every visible signal said success: the append returned a record, the fold returned `effective statement recorded`, and the commitment moved to reported. Only a projection query against `.reviews` reveals the act did not become what it was written to be. This is the same shape as the missing-`conditions` trap, where a request lands effective-looking and is ruled ineffective for a missing body field — except here the act is genuinely effective, just not the kind of thing its author meant.\n\nI do not have a preferred remedy and the choice is a real design question, so I am stating the problem rather than the fix. Candidates: warn when a report body carries a verdict-like key the fold does not read; reject `status` on a report kind outright; have the MCP tool accept the same vocabulary the CLI enforces; or surface, in the tool response, what the act projected as rather than only that it landed.","body":{"conditions":"Filing a review through the MCP `state` tool either cannot silently fail to become a review, or tells its author that it did not: the chosen remedy is implemented and covered by a regression that files a report whose body names a verdict-like key the fold does not read and asserts the author is told; the existing CLI behaviour and the fold's `verdict` contract are unchanged, or changed deliberately with the reason recorded; the trade-off between rejecting unknown body keys and preserving the open body map is stated in the design note or the reference page, since refusing unknown keys narrows a deliberately open structure; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}